 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the social services law, in relation to publication of a child support standards chart; to amend the social services law and the family court act, in relation to clarifying the role of the support collection unit in violation proceedings and the role of an attorney appearing for the social services district on behalf of an individual receiving child support services; to amend the family court act, in relation to correcting references to federal authority; and to amend the social services law, the family court act and the domestic relations law, in relation to increasing the combined parental income amount utilized for the calculation of child support",
    "basePrintNo": "A8888",
    "basePrintNoStr": "A8888-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§111-i & 111-c, Soc Serv L; amd §§453, 516-a & 413, Fam Ct Act; amd §240, Dom Rel L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A8888\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the social services law, in relation to\npublication of a child support standards chart; to amend the social\nservices law and the family court act, in relation to clarifying the\nrole of the support collection unit in violation proceedings and the\nrole of an attorney appearing for the social services district on behalf\nof an individual receiving child support services; to amend the family\ncourt act, in relation to correcting references to federal authority;\nand to amend the social services law, the family court act and the\ndomestic relations law, in relation to increasing the combined parental\nincome amount utilized for the calculation of child support\n\nPurpose of the bill:  This bill would: (1) clarify and confirm the role\nof the child support enforcement unit relating to the provision of child\nsupport enforcement services to individual parties; (2) ensure consist-\nency between Social Services Law and the federal Office of Child Support\nEnforcement, the oversight agency for state child support enforcement\nprograms operating in compliance with Title IV-D of the Federal Social\nSecurity Act (\"Title IV-D\") by providing that attorneys providing legal\nservices under Title IV-D represent the IV-D agency in carrying out its\nduties; (3) correct a reference to federal law in Family Court Act (FCA)\n§ 413; (4) remove a requirement that certain charts published annually\nby the Commissioner of OTDA must appear in regulation; and (5) increase\nthe consistency of support orders throughout the State by updating the\ncombined parental income amount utilized by courts for calculating\nsupport to reflect current economic realities.\n\nSummary of provisions:  Section 1 of the bill would give the bill the\nshort title of the \"Child Support Modernization Act.\"\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend Social Services Law (SSL) § 111-i (2)\nto: (1) remove the requirement that publication of the self-support\nreserve and the child support standards chart published by the Commis-\nsioner of OTDA appear in regulation; and (2) define the combined\nparental income amount and require publication thereof in the child\nsupport standards chart.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would amend SSL § 111-c to provide that: (1) in\nproviding child support services, a local social services district\nrepresents the interests of the district in carrying out its duties\nunder the child support program; (2) the provision of legal services\nunder the child support program does not constitute or create an attor-\nney-client relationship between the individual receiving services and\nany attorney representing or appearing for the district to provide such\nlegal services; (3) the social services district has standing to appear\nin any child support proceeding involving an individual in receipt of\nchild support services; and (4) the social services district will\nprovide notice to individuals requesting or receiving services that the\nattorney representing or appearing for the district does not represent\nthe individual.\n\nSection 4 of the bill would amend FCA § 453 (a) to clarify that the\nsupport collection unit may originate and prosecute violation petitions\non behalf of persons in receipt of public assistance or in receipt of\nservices pursuant to SSL § 111-g.\n\nSection 5 of the bill would amend FCA § 516-a to correct a reference to\nfederal law.\n\nSections 6 and 7 of the bill would amend FCA § 413 and Domestic\nRelations Law (DRL) § 240, respectively, to increase from $80,000 to\n$130,000 the combined parental income amount used to calculate child\nsupport orders and for which application of the Child Support Standards\nAct (CSSA) percentages are mandatory. These sections also include a\nmechanism for revising the \"combined parental income amount\" every other\nyear based upon the consumer price index for all urban consumers (CPI-U)\npublished by the United States Department of Labor.\n\nSection 8 of the bill would provide for an immediate effective date for\nall sections of the bill, except for sections 2, 6 and 7 which would\ntake effect on January 31, 2010.\n\nExisting law:  Various sections of the FCA, the DRL and the SSL, includ-\ning FCA § 413 and DRL § 240, contain provisions concerning the obli-\ngation of parents to support their children.\n\nSSL § 111-c sets forth the functions, powers and duties of social\nservices districts.\n\nSSL § 111-i requires the commissioner of OTDA to publish annually in\nregulations the child support standards chart.\n\nFCA § 453 provides that the support collection unit may originate a\npetition for a violation of a court order.\n\nFCA § 516-a sets out the procedures for establishing paternity by writ-\nten voluntary acknowledgement.\n\nPrior legislative history:  In the 2007 legislative session, S.4848\ncontained provisions similar to sections 2 and 3 of this bill. The bill\nwas not reported from the Senate Judiciary Committee. In the 2008 legis-\nlative session, similar provisions were part of S.8340 which was not\nreported from the Senate Rules Committee.\n\nStatement in support:  Under Title IV-D, child support enforcement\nservices must be made available by the local district child support\nenforcement units (CSEU) to both custodial and non-custodial parents,\npublic assistance recipients and non-public assistance recipients, and\nmust be conducted in accordance with applicable State and federal laws\nand regulations. Likewise, legal services must be made available by the\nCSEU to all persons in receipt of public assistance and care and, upon\nrequest only, to persons in receipt of services pursuant to SSL § 111-g.\nHowever, existing law does not clearly provide that the role of the\n\nCSEU, including that of its legal staff, is to comply with State and\nfederal program requirements and provide enforcement-related services to\nindividuals and not to represent an individual in a traditional attor-\nney-client relationship. The bill would clarify and confirm the role of\nthe CSEU in providing child support enforcement services, thereby ensur-\ning the program will comply with federal requirements.\n\nAs originally conceived in the 1970s, child support enforcement programs\noperating in compliance with Title IV-D were responsible for establish-\ning paternity and obtaining support for custodial parents.  Over the\nlast several decades, the Title IV-D program has expanded to require\nstate certified programs to provide services to both custodial and non-\ncustodial parents and the caseload composition has changed from predomi-\nnantly public assistance families to former or never-assistance fami-\nlies. Attorneys providing legal services are generally government\nemployees (county attorneys or local county department of social\nservices employees) or are under contract with local governments. In\norder to fulfill this expanded role and ensure compliance with attorney\nethical obligations, the child support enforcement attorney's role has\nbeen clarified in states nationwide to provide that the IV-D attorney\nand the IV-D staff do not represent individual parties but instead\nrepresent the agency with respect to the delivery of IV-D services,\nspecifically establishing paternity and establishing, modifying and\nenforcing orders of support. The bill would clarify that attorneys who\nprovide child support services do not represent individual parties, but\nrepresent the agency in the provision of these services.\n\nThe bill would also clarify that the support collection unit may not\nonly originate but may also prosecute a violation proceeding on behalf\nof non-public assistance individuals in receipt of child support\nservices pursuant to SSL § 111-g. This amendment is in response to\nMONROE COUNTY SUPPORT COLLECTION UNIT v. WHITE, 2 A.D.3d 1368 (4th Dept.\n2003), which held that: (1) while the support collection unit had\nauthority under FCA § 453 to originate the proceeding, it lacked author-\nity to prosecute a violation proceeding regarding an order payable\nthrough the support collection unit for the support of a child not\nreceiving public assistance; and (2) the support collection unit lacked\nstanding to prosecute the violation proceedings because the custodial\nparent did not execute a \"right to recovery\" agreement permitting the\nsupport collection unit to recover its costs, and therefore was not a\n\"party in interest.\"\n\nThe bill would also eliminate the requirement that the Commissioner of\nOTDA publish the revised self-support reserve and child support stand-\nards chart in regulation. The Commissioner would still publish this\ninformation, but it would appear in a document produced by OTDA.\n\nThe bill also corrects a citation error contained in FCA § 516-a. FCA\n516-a (d) erroneously refers to section 652 of the Social Security Act.\nSection 652 (a) (7) is the U.S. Code section; section 452 is the rele-\nvant section of the Social Security Act.\n\nLastly, the bill would increase the \"combined parental income amount\"\n(the maximum dollar value of parental income, or \"cap\", to which the\nChild Support Standards Act (CSSA) percentages must be applied for the\ncalculation of child support) from $80,000 to $130,000 upon the effec-\ntive date of the proposal, with a mechanism beginning in 2012 for revis-\ning this threshold every two years to reflect changes in the consumer\nprice index. The current threshold of $80,000 was created in 1989. Since\nthen, there have been both increases in income levels and the cost of\nliving, thus it no longer signifies the high-income families it was\nintended to delineate, families with combined income levels potentially\nwarranting deviation from the CSSA percentages. In short, the exception\nhas lost its meaning. It is imperative that the \"combined parental\nincome amount\" keep pace with current economic realities so that proper\norders are granted on a consistent and predictable basis throughout the\nState, leaving only exceptional income cases to potentially be deter-\nmined outside of the presumptively correct CSSA percentages.\n\nBudget implications:  This bill would have no significant fiscal impli-\ncations.\n\nEffective date:  This bill would provide for an immediate effective\ndate, except for sections 2, 6 and 7 which would take effect January 31,\n2010.\n\n",
